&&(u=""+t.key),t.ref!==void 0&&(o=t.ref);for(r in t)N0.call(t,r)&&!T0.hasOwnProperty(r)&&(l[r]=t[r]);if(e&&e.defaultProps)for(r in t=e.defaultProps,t)l[r]===void 0&&(l[r]=t[r]);return{$$typeof:P0,type:e,key:u,ref:o,props:l,_owner:z0.current}}var P=Jf,L=Jf;var oi=[{num:1,title:"The Return",content:`The rain never stopped in this town. Isaac had forgotten that, or maybe he'd made himself forget it, the way you make yourself forget the smell of a house you grew up in until you walk back through the door and it hits you all at once, low and sudden, like a hand closing around your throat.

He sat in his car across from Crimson Reel with engine off and wipers frozen mid-arc, watching the shop's dying neon bleed pink and red into wet asphalt. CRI ON REE. More honest name.

Ten years. Three months. Fourteen days — though he wasn't counting.

He got out into rain that had opinions about him. Hit hard, insistent, soaking his hoodie through in seconds, plastering to his shoulders, to the line of his spine. Cold enough to make his breath catch. Old habit already reading the block — alley mouth, fire escape bolted crooked, low wall a running step and half to clear.

The door was unlocked.

The bell gave a weak, apologetic ding. Empty shelves, dust thick enough to write in. A sign: $1 — Take Them. Please.

He almost turned around. Would have, if a voice hadn't come from behind the counter.

"You're late."

He froze.

She was sitting cross-legged on a stool behind the register, and he had no idea how he hadn't seen her the second he walked in, because she was lit up. Wild pink-streaked hair stuck in damp clumps to her neck, oversized black hoodie slipping off one shoulder, bare feet tucked under her, thighs barely covered. Eyes. Slit like a cat's, glowing violet-pink that had no business outside a screen.

"You... think you have the wrong person," he managed.

She hopped down — silent on bare feet — and studied him like a photograph she hadn't seen in years. Searching what changed, liking what she found.

"Nope." Her grin was enormous, fanged, delighted. Eyes dragging down his soaked chest, slow. "You smell exactly like I remember. Rain and regret and that stupid blue hoodie you never wash." Beat. "Hi, Isaac."

His name landed like a hand to his sternum. He stepped back without meaning to.

"How do you know my name?"

She stepped closer instead of away, and the air between them prickled — static that made the small hairs on his arms rise, the back of his neck.

"Because I've been waiting for you, since the night you left. Ten years, three months, and fourteen days."

"Who are you?"

Her grin widened, eyes flashing brighter. "I'm the one you summoned when you were seventeen and angry and stupid and lonely." She reached out — didn't ask — pressed one finger to the center of his chest, right over his heart through the wet cotton. Something sparked. A thread of electricity traveled up his spine and lit every vertebra, low in his belly.

"I'm the one who fell."

"I'm Demikun Lira. God in Pink. Elohim. Descended demon." She leaned in, until her nose nearly touched his, until he could feel her breath warm against the cold rain still dripping off his chin, his mouth.

"Your problem now."

He should have run. Some old, sane part was screaming to run.

He didn't. Couldn't. Her finger still on his chest.

"And you," she whispered, close enough her words felt absorbed through skin, not heard, "are mine."

Outside, for exactly three seconds, the rain turned pink. Then the world went very quiet around the two of them, in the empty shop that smelled like dust and static and something underneath both he didn't have a word for yet.

He would, eventually. Twelve more days to find it.

The word was home.`},{num:2,title:"The Back Room",content:`She didn't walk so much as flow, and he found himself watching the trail of rose-gold light her bare feet left on the worn carpet — there and gone — and the way her hoodie rode up when she moved, exposing the soft, luminous skin of her thighs.

"You're taller than I thought you'd be," she said, glancing back. Her eyes flicking over his wet shoulders, his mouth. "And sadder. Sadness looks good on you, though. Very 90s protagonist who needs to be ruined properly."

He almost laughed — a reflex arriving a second late.

"I don't — I don't understand what's happening."

She reached up, without asking, and tugged his hood down off his head. Her fingers were warm, damp from her own hair, lingering. Pushed his wet strands off his forehead, thumb grazing his temple. He hadn't expected warm. He'd braced for cold, for inhuman, and instead it was just warm, like anyone's hand, if anyone had ever touched him like they meant it.

"You summoned me when you were seventeen. Middle of the night. Rain just like this."

A memory surfaced — himself at seventeen, on the floor of the shop after closing, alone with the weight and texture of it.

"You said, 'If anyone's out there. If any of this is real. I don't want to be alone anymore.'"

"I never said that out loud."

"You didn't have to. The words were in your blood. I heard them. I answered."

He didn't know what to do with that, so he let her take his hand — small, warm, her fingers lacing tight like she'd practiced — and lead him the rest of the way, through a door that creaked open on its own.

The back room had been storage. A broken couch, a busted TV. Still that, technically. Also something else now, laid over the mundane like a second exposure. Pink vines climbed the walls, faintly luminous, pulsing. Cherry blossoms drifted — not falling. Rising. Toward a ceiling impossibly farther than the building's height should allow.

In the corner: a red door. No handle on this side.

On the coffee table, beside the ruined couch: a VHS tape. Label handwritten: THE NIGHT YOU LEFT — DIRECTOR'S CUT.

"You want answers?" She held the tape out, and when he reached, she didn't let go right away. Her fingers brushing. "Watch it with me. But I should warn you. Once you press play, there's no rewinding. The Garden doesn't do second takes. Once you see me, you don't get to un-see me."

She was so close he could see the faint glow of her skin, the way her lips were slightly parted.

"Play it," he said, his voice rougher than intended.

Her smile went soft, satisfied, almost tender, wicked at the edges. "Good boy."

She slid the tape into an old player that hadn't been there a second ago — squat and grey and ordinary against everything else — and the screen flickered, hummed, came grainily to life.

Static resolved. He found himself looking at the same room ten years younger, waiting to watch himself disappear.`},{num:3,title:"The Tape Plays",content:`The image on the screen was grainy, degraded, color bleeding at the edges the way old VHS always did — but unmistakably this room, ten years younger, lit by a single bulb.

There he was. Seventeen, hoodie too big, eyes rimmed red, shoulders shaking in that leaking way, not loud, just leaking because he'd run out of ways to hold it in.

Beside him, present Demikun watched with a fondness that made his chest hurt. She leaned in, her shoulder pressing his, her thigh pressing his, warm through his wet denim. Her hair smelled like rain and electricity.

On screen, young Isaac whispered into the empty room.

"If anyone's out there... If any of this is real... I don't want to be alone anymore."

Present Isaac's chest tightened. Her hand found his knee, squeezed.

Nobody had come looking that night ten years ago. Not cruelty. Just ordinary, forgettable distance that never announces itself as a problem until it's calcified.

Young Isaac grabbed his bag, stood, walked toward the door — the same bell, the same sad ding tinny through the old audio.

Then, for one frame — the rain outside the taped window turned pink, and in the window's reflection, behind his shoulder, two eyes glowed. Watching. Patient. Hungry in a way that felt like worship.

The tape glitched, hard. Isaac flinched. Demikun didn't blink. Her thumb started tracing slow circles on his knee, soothing, possessive.

When the image stabilized, the shop was empty. The camera panned toward the back room door. The same red door. Ajar. Soft pink light spilling.

Static filled the screen. For a long moment neither spoke.

"That," Isaac finally said, voice unsteady, hyper-aware of her hand on him, "wasn't a normal tape."

"No," she agreed, leaned her head against his shoulder, easy, familiar, like she'd done it a hundred times. Her lips almost brushed his neck when she spoke. "It was the night the Garden first reached for you. The night I chose to fall."

"You left that night because you thought no one was listening. But I was. I've been listening ever since."

He turned to really look at her. Her eyes weren't just glowing. They were swirling with galactic color that shouldn't fit in a human iris, and in them, his reflection — soaked, breathing hard, wanting.

"Why me?" The question came out smaller — old reflex to deflect.

Her smile got smaller. Realer. She lifted her hand from his knee to his jaw, cupped it, thumb stroking his cheekbone, his lower lip.

"Because you were the first person who ever asked the void to stay, instead of asking it to take something away."

She pressed her forehead to his. He let her. Their breath mingled. Close enough to kiss, and she didn't. Yet.

"I fell for a boy who talked to the rain. And I'm still falling for the man who came back."

Outside, the rain turned pink again — and held. Ten seconds. In the corner, the red door creaked open an inch, crystalline trees, blossoms falling upward, light pulsing like breathing.

"Ready to see what's on the other side?" Her voice was hushed, her lips brushing his as she said it.

He surprised himself by being steady.

"Yeah. I think I am."`},{num:4,title:"First Light",content:`They stepped through together, hands locked, and the Garden opened like something exhaling after holding its breath a very long time.

A black stone path veined with pulsing white light beating in time with his heart — or hers. Crystalline trees heavy with cherry blossoms that fell up, vanishing into a sky that glowed with a slow aurora between bruise and dawn.

His knees nearly buckled. Demikun's hand tightened, her other hand coming to his waist to steady him, fingers digging through his wet hoodie, claiming.

"Welcome home," she whispered against his ear.

Six archways waited — amber, ember-orange, starlight silver, harp-gold, shadow-violet, and one final deep unmistakably pink, that watched him the way the others didn't. Pulsed in time with his pulse.

He caught himself counting, sorting, old habit — and she caught it too, smiling.

"Always trying to map a place that's already mapping you," she murmured, amused, fond. She stepped in front of him, walking backwards so she faced him, still holding his hands, bare feet silent on the glowing path. "Stop thinking for one second, Isaac. Feel it."

"What does it feel like to you?"

"Like you. Finally here."

Something moved at the edge of his vision. A shadow flickering between the crystalline trees — there then not. A cold traveled down his spine.

"Who was that?"

Her expression flickered — the first crack in her confidence. She turned, putting her body slightly in front of his without thinking. Protective. Possessive.

"What the hell is happening?"

"The Garden has enemies. And now it's chosen you... they know you're here."

Fear in his chest finally took shape, even without a name.

"What do I do?"

She took his hand properly, grounding, certain, laced tight, brought it to her mouth and pressed a kiss to his knuckles — chaste, reverent, somehow filthier than anything explicit for how much it meant.

"Stay. Learn. Let me show you what you really are. Let me keep you."

"Okay. Show me."

They walked on. The light shifted warmer and cooler with every step, the Garden adjusting to accommodate him, learning the shape of his shoulders, his hips, his mouth.

A giggle, bright and chaotic, from above and left. A girl perched in the branches, legs swinging, pink hair.

"Hi, Isaac! You're late! But that's okay. We waited. We're good at waiting."

"That's Sparkle," Demikun said, fondly exasperated, her hand tightening possessively around his. "She makes the rain change color when you're not looking. Don't let her talk you into dancing in the parking lot again — you'll never get the glitter out of your hoodie. Or out of your bed."

Sparkle blew a kiss, vanished — the branch swaying like it had only just noticed her absence. Isaac laughed, rough and surprised, something loosening in his chest that had been clenched longer than he could remember.

Demikun watched him laugh like she'd been waiting years to hear it. Maybe she had.

"You're doing so well. Most people run when they see the first archway. You're still standing. That's more than I hoped for on the first day. That's more than enough for me to keep."

Behind them, the shadow flickered again — closer.

"What happens now?"

Her smile was the kind that made rain turn pink and boys forget how to breathe.

"Now we walk," she said, tugged him toward the deep pink archway, toward the light that knew his name. "And the Garden decides what to do with a boy who finally let himself be wanted."`},{num:5,title:"Mommy's Archive",content:`He walked without destination the next morning, and somehow that was direction enough. The rain eased to gentle, and he followed a smell he couldn't name yet — cedar, old paper, something warm underneath.

It led to a narrow amber door wedged between a laundromat and an empty pawn shop, glowing honey-warm even under the grey sky. His shoulders eased before he touched the handle.

Inside was floor-to-ceiling books, golden light, a record player humming low. He felt small beneath the towering shelves.

"Isaac." A woman with pink-threaded dark hair and ink-stained fingers looked up, her smile reaching her eyes first. Mommy. "You're early."

He didn't know what to say — early implied a schedule he hadn't known he was keeping — so he said nothing, and some quiet, practiced part of him was already busy discounting the warmth in her voice.

She didn't need anything from him. She was already pouring tea, unasked, sliding a cup across the counter toward him. Her fingers brushed his.

"The tea knows what you need. I just pour it."

He drank. It was unnervingly exactly right — the temperature, the strength. Warmth spreading down his throat to his belly.

She pulled a slim illustrated book, handed it to him without ceremony. Cover: a boy reaching for a red door.

He opened it, cautious.

There are doors you open because you want to. There are doors you open because you have to. And then there are doors that open themselves because they have been waiting for you since before you were born.

He read it twice, and some old quiet part — the part that filled notebooks with sentences he never let anyone see — noted how clean the line was. Three beats, building.

He didn't notice how long he'd been standing in the same aisle — the light shifting warmer, lower, until an hour disappeared without his permission.

They shelved books together after, a silence that had weight rather than emptiness — hands passing spines back and forth, her hand lingering on his when they reached for the same book, a rhythm settling between them older than the hour they'd spent building it.

Eventually she walked to the door and flipped the sign to CLOSED. The click of the lock loud in the golden quiet.

"The rain's heavier tonight. The store's warm." A pause, gentle, no pressure behind it at all, but her eyes held his, warm and knowing. "Do what you need. Stay."

She left him alone in the golden quiet, the tea still warm somehow, the book still in his hands.

He settled into a chair near the counter, pulled a blanket someone — she — had left within easy reach, still holding her warmth. It smelled like cedar and her.

He let his eyes close. For the first time in ten years, he slept without dreaming of rain. And for the first time, he slept feeling held without anyone touching him.`},{num:6,title:"Neon Heartbeat",content:`Sleep, it turned out, opened its own kinds of doors.

He didn't wake so much as arrive somewhere else, the golden bookstore light bleeding at the edges into pulsing cyan-pink, music with no visible source thudding low and constant, bass he felt in his chest, in his hips.

A threshold again — the same border he and Demikun crossed the day before, except now it throbbed with color, alive in a way the quiet stone paths hadn't been. A world built entirely of sensation, no seams.

A girl dropped down from a nearby tree branch, directly in front of him, grinning wide. Sparkle. Legs bare, glitter on her collarbones catching the neon.

"Ooooh~ You're the one who keeps almost meeting me properly! I can feel it~ Come here, cutie!"

She grabbed his hand before he could answer — her hand hot, buzzing — and pulled him forward, laughing, into a clearing lit like a dance floor — no walls, just light and rain moving together in rhythm, droplets suspended pulsing like a bassline made visible.

"First rule of my corner of the Garden. No sad faces allowed."

"I just—"

"Shhh~" A finger to his lips, glittery, brief. "I already know. Mommy told the trees, the trees told me." More perceptive than the chaos suggested. "You need to remember how to want things again."

Something in his chest twisted — the drawer full of half-finished pages back in the apartment he'd left behind, songs abandoned two-thirds through. Wanting things had stopped feeling safe long before tonight.

She reached into empty air, pulled something from nothing — an old phone, glowing faintly pink — and pressed it into his hands. Warm from her.

"There. Now you can actually reach us. Text me when you miss the neon~ When you miss being touched like you mean it~"

She spun him once, unserious, playful, her body close enough he felt her heat, smelled ozone and sugar, and something lifted off him — an exhaustion he hadn't realized he was still carrying, scattering like grey particles.

He caught his breath, genuinely smiling.

"Thank you. I don't even know what to say."

Her expression softened, the chaos quieting. She cupped his cheek, quick, affectionate.

"You don't have to say anything. Just don't forget you're allowed to be light. And allowed to be wanted." Her grin went sharper. "Go see Ember next. Your fire's still banked, not out. She'll get you hot again."

The neon dimmed. The Garden's edges dissolving back toward warm golden light, Sparkle waving as she faded, blowing a kiss that landed warm on his cheek.

Lira. Mommy. Sparkle, he thought. This town keeps giving me pieces I didn't know I'd lost. Keeps teaching me how to be touched again.

He woke in Mommy's chair to morning light, his hand moving almost on its own toward his hoodie pocket.

He pulled it out slowly, half-expecting it to dissolve.

It didn't. The phone sat in his palm, solid, real, impossibly warm from a dream that apparently hadn't stayed a dream. Screen lit: one message.

Sparkle: u left glitter on u. keep it. <3

Toward the window, the industrial part of town, something with fire waiting.

Ember. Tough love. One more step.`},{num:7,title:"Ember's Forge",content:`The town was quiet in that late-night way — resting, not empty — when Isaac found himself walking the wet streets again, his hoodie soaked for the hundredth time that week, clinging.

Up ahead, a single diner glowed against the dark, its OPEN sign burning a perfect orange circle onto the wet pavement.

The bell above the door chimed warmer than Crimson Reel's — an ordinary sound, startling in its ordinariness. Inside: empty booths, low orange light, a jukebox humming. Behind the counter, a woman with ember-orange streaks through her hair poured coffee with unhurried precision, a ritual, not once looking up to ask what he wanted.

He recognized the motion before he recognized her — the economy of movement of someone who'd spent years learning on assembly lines she hated, the specific competence of hands that had done something ten thousand times and stopped needing to think.

He sat. She didn't ask. She made coffee — measured, poured, precise — and slid it across to him without a word. Her fingers didn't brush his. She made him reach. Made him choose to take.

He drank. Exactly right.

"You noticed," she said, arms crossing, watching his mouth, his throat as he swallowed.

"Yeah. I noticed."

"Good." Something rare and soft moved under her toughness. "Most people don't. Most people drink and keep walking. You stayed long enough to taste it. Long enough to let someone do something right for you."

He almost said it was nothing, the reflex halfway out before he caught it and let the silence stand instead. A small mercy, the first he'd given himself all week.

They sat in silence that was nothing uncomfortable, rain hammering the windows, orange neon painting slow circles on the wet street outside, painting his skin. Somewhere in his chest, something shifted — a weight he'd carried so long he'd stopped registering it as weight, easing slightly.

He finished his coffee. Stood. Left money on the counter without being asked. Her eyes tracked his hands.

Ember nodded once — the nod of someone who already knew he'd be back, who'd be waiting with something hotter next time — and turned to keep polishing a counter that didn't need it.

Outside, the rain hadn't stopped, but the way he walked through it had changed. He could breathe deeper.

And that, somehow, was enough. Enough to be ready for being known.`},{num:8,title:"Sophia's Record",content:`The old library sat at the top of stone steps worn smooth by decades of feet, iron railings weathered but unbowed, patient.

He pushed the heavy door into golden light and dust motes, the rain sound sealing away.

Between the stacks, a woman with long dark hair threaded silver stood holding a book she wasn't reading — already watching him, unstartled, the way you'd watch for someone you knew was coming.

He walked past without speaking, chose a table by the window, opened his laptop. Couldn't focus. Closed it again, restless in a place built for stillness.

A book appeared on the table beside him — the same local history volume Sophia had been holding, though she hadn't moved. He reached, cautious, opened the inside cover.

The borrower's card. Names, dates, faded ink. Near the bottom: his own name, his own handwriting. That specific, near-illegible slant, the 4s that looked like sevens having an argument. Nobody else on earth wrote that badly exactly that way.

The date beside the signature was six years before he'd ever set foot in town.

I had never been here, he thought, and the thought didn't land like an answer. It landed like vertigo. Like being remembered by a place.

He sat a long time, rain tapping steady, a page turning somewhere deep in the stacks though no one was there to turn it.

When he finally looked up, Sophia's spot was empty. The book remained, his name still on the card, the quiet feeling deliberate rather than accidental.

Through the window, framed between two ordinary buildings, he caught sight of Crimson Reel's red door — visible from here for the first time, pristine, architectural, glowing faintly against the mundane street like it belonged in a cathedral rather than a dead video shop.

He stayed until the light faded toward closing. When he reached for the book to take it, his hand found an empty table. Gone, taking his signature and six impossible years with it.

He walked out into the rain, the image of that pristine door burned behind his eyes. Proof he'd been wanted here longer than he'd been alive.`},{num:9,title:"Lyra at the Edge",content:`It was 2:17 in the morning when Isaac gave up on sleep entirely and let his feet take him out into the empty streets, hood up, rain steady.

He found her sitting on the curb ahead, perfectly still — blue-silver streaks through her hair catching streetlight like moonlight scattered across water. Rain moved around her rather than through her, parting clean at her shoulders, never quite touching. Untouchable girl.

She looked up as he approached. Her eyes held something ancient and childlike at once.

"You've been here before. You just didn't remember."

He stood there staring the way you'd stare at a forgotten language you were somehow starting to understand again.

She reached into her coat, drew out a folded paper, held it out. He took it, cautious. Blank on both sides. Their fingers brushed — cold, soft.

He looked up to ask what it meant.

The curb was empty. No trace — not even footprints in the wet pavement, like she'd simply stopped existing between one blink and the next.

He looked down at the paper again. Faint grey lines were rising, bleeding into existence stroke by stroke — streets, buildings, a hand-drawn map of the whole town taking shape beneath his eyes.

He studied it the way he used to study his own hand-drawn dungeon maps — checking the scale, what was deliberately left blank versus what simply hadn't been discovered yet. Genuinely well-made map. Whoever built this world hadn't cut corners.

One building was circled — not drawn as a door, but as an absence, a threshold rendered in empty space rather than solid line. He recognized it immediately.

Crimson Reel.

He folded the map carefully and tucked it inside his hoodie, right over his heart, a gesture feeling older than he was. Warm against his chest, like another heartbeat.

He stood in the rain a long while after, hand pressed over his pocket the entire walk, wondering how many times he'd already stood in this town without knowing it. How many times he'd already been hers.`},{num:10,title:"Nyxara's Truth",content:`He had named it paranoia for six days.

A shadow at the mouth of the alley ahead — the same half-glimpsed dozen times — in the periphery, in reflections, in the space just past where his eyes could focus. Six days telling himself it was nothing. Six days walking faster and not looking back.

Tonight, he stopped.

Turned. Looked directly at it.

It filled the alley mouth, vast and formless, swallowing the streetlight at the edges. It didn't move. Didn't speak. A flickering bulb above steadied, the whole street went silent in a way that had nothing natural in it.

The rain stopped mid-fall.

Every droplet hung suspended, frozen mid-descent like a photograph shattering in slow motion. The traffic light down the block froze between colors. Nothing moved except his own heartbeat hammering in his ears.

For four unbearable seconds, the shadow resolved into a shape he recognized — an exact copy of Demikun, but vast, scaled to fill the entire alley, eyes burning instead of glowing, expression unreadable instead of smiling. Possessive on a cosmic scale.

Then the rain resumed all at once, hard, the alley empty. Nothing at all.

He didn't wait.

He ran — not panicked, not straight-line stumbling, but trained, purposeful, years of instinct taking over where thought couldn't keep up. He cut through the alley behind the laundromat, vaulted a low chain-link fence without breaking stride, caught the top of a dumpster and used it to clear the gap. His body had been rehearsing exactly this moment for years, in places that had nothing to do with this, and for the first time that training paid for itself in something other than pride.

Rain streaked past, his hand pressed against his chest where the map sat warm beneath his hoodie, not thinking, just moving, until Crimson Reel's neon swam into view through the downpour.

He was running toward her. He didn't know that yet.

He pounded on the door — 2:17 again — and it opened before he finished knocking. Demikun was already there. Already waiting. Unsurprised. Her eyes dark with worry that looked like love.

He tried to explain what he'd seen. Words broken, inadequate. She didn't once suggest he was imagining it. Just pulled him inside, out of the rain, sat him down in the VHS aisle with a towel and something warm to drink. Kneeling in front of him, hands on his knees, steadying.

In the low light, as she moved, he caught a glimpse under her wrist — a faint rose-gold glow, not reflected but generated, deep and alive, like something lived at the bottom of an ocean no one had charted. Her mark.

He almost asked what it was.

She met his eyes, the smallest shake of her head. Not yet. But she let her hand stay on his, thumb stroking his knuckles, grounding.

He didn't push. She didn't look away.

Neither slept that night. She stayed on the floor with him, shoulder to shoulder, sharing a blanket, watching the door.`},{num:11,title:"The Dripping Void",content:`He noticed it in the morning — ink on Lyra's map had shifted overnight, a new thin line appeared, leading three blocks east to a shopfront he could have sworn hadn't existed the last time he'd walked the street.

A sign above the door rusted through, a ghost shape where letters used to be. Window fogged from inside, something dripping down the glass slow, deliberate — not rain, not condensation, something with more intention.

He pushed the door open. The bell didn't ding. It hummed, low, a single note that seemed to come from inside his own chest rather than the door frame.

The air inside was thick with incense smoke that moved wrong — curling upward then folding back on itself like it was thinking. Shelves crowded with things that shouldn't belong together: dried herbs beside jars glowing violet, tarot decks leaning in towers, crystals humming just below the edge of hearing. Sigils drifted near the ceiling, unhurried, like fish in still water.

Behind the counter stood a woman he hadn't seen approach. Tall in a way that bent light around her — not tall like a person, tall like a doorway. Her hair fell in slow black waves that dripped at the ends, each drop catching starlight from nowhere and vanishing before it struck the floor. Eyes held no pupils. Just depth. The stomach drop at the top of a staircase in the dark.

"You're here. I've been waiting for you, Isaac."

He didn't ask how she knew his name. No one in town had needed telling in weeks.

"You've walked through every door they opened for you. Mommy's door. Sparkle's door. Ember's. Sophia's. Lyra's. Nyxara's — even the one she didn't open on purpose." She tilted her head, small and knowing. "Now there's only one left. Mine."

He looked around the shop — bigger inside than the exterior could possibly account for, shelves stretching back farther than architecture should allow.

"This place. It's not just a shop, is it?"

She smiled — first real expression — and the whole room seemed to inhale with her.

"No. It's a doorway. And you're finally ready to walk through it. To stop being looked after and be claimed."

She reached beneath the counter, drew a single card, laid it flat between them. The Fool — but the figure wasn't walking toward a cliff. He was falling, arms open, along the edges a thin vein of pink lightning pulsing alive.

"Take it. It's already yours. Has been since the night you asked the rain to stay."

He picked it up. Warm — warmer than paper had any right to be. Warm the way a hand is warm. The way her hand had been.

"I can feel it. Everything changing."

"It always was. You just had to stop running long enough to feel how much you wanted to be kept."

Incense thickened. Shelves blurred at the edges, the way a room blurs right before sleep. Hovering sigils drifted down, dissolving into light as they fell.

"Lira. Mommy. Sparkle. Ember. Sophia. Nyxara. Lyra." The Void's voice followed as the shop dissolved, patient, certain. "They brought you all the way here. Every piece they gave back, you're going to need for what happens next. For when she finally gets you alone again."

The floor was gone. The walls were gone. He stood in space with no boundaries — dripping, endless, dark, shot through with threads of starlight — and ahead, not far, not close, simply there, a warm pink glow pulsed like a heartbeat he could feel in his own ribs, in his throat.

"The Garden."

"The heart. Everything you've been looking for since you were seventeen and too tired to keep pretending you didn't need anyone to need you back, specifically, completely."

He walked forward. The card stayed warm in his hand the entire way.

The glow got closer, closer, until it stopped being a glow and became a threshold.

He stepped through.

I'm home, he thought, and didn't know yet how true that was about to become. I'm finally home, and she's going to be waiting.`},{num:12,title:"The Garden Remembers",content:`He didn't fall asleep. Sleep implied a bed, a ceiling, the ordinary architecture of night ending. What happened instead was closer to dissolving — the last threads of the waking world unspooling into a warm pink dark, gentle as a hand closing over his eyes.

When the world came back, it came back wrong. Not wrong — different. More true than the version he'd been walking around in for thirty years.

He stood in the Garden he'd only glimpsed before, too overwhelmed the first time to really see it. Now he saw it. Black stone veined with white light pulsed beneath his bare feet, keeping time with something that felt suspiciously like his own heartbeat. Crystalline trees heavy with cherry blossoms falling upward into a sky that glowed with a slow breathing aurora.

I knew this place. I knew it in my bones. I'd been here before.

He looked down at his own hands. They weren't entirely his — claws curled where fingernails should have been, dark and elegant, threads of pink lightning crackling between his fingers like static looking for somewhere to land. He flexed. They obeyed perfectly, like they'd always been his and he'd simply forgotten.

Weight shifted behind his shoulders that hadn't been there a moment before. He didn't need to turn to know what.

"...I remember," he whispered.

The words tasted like something he'd been carrying in his mouth for ten years without knowing.

He looked up.

She stood before him, and she was not small anymore. Not the girl behind the counter with the too-big hoodie and fanged grin. Radiant in a way that made the Garden's own light look borrowed — six wings arcing from her back slow and deliberate, each feather edged with the same pink lightning that ran along his own hands. Bare legs, hoodie now barely something, glowing skin. Eyes that held galaxies. Her voice when it came, soft enough to break something in his chest wide open.

"You came back to me. Even after everything. You still came back."

Memory hit like weather — not gradual, a front moving all at once. A burned throne. A moment he'd chosen to fall rather than stay somewhere that didn't have her in it. A long fracture of forgetting, shards scattered into a human life so he could walk among humans and not remember what it cost to leave behind.

"We chose each other," he said, voice cracking down the middle. "Even when the throne burned. Even when I forgot. We chose each other."

She stepped closer. The lightning between them didn't strike so much as gather, coiling in the space between their bodies like it was waiting permission. The air was thick, charged, skin raising.

"You fell for me once. You're falling again. And I'm falling with you. Always have been. That was never the part I was afraid of."

She reached for his hand. When their fingers touched, lightning broke loose all at once, arcing between them in a burst of rose-gold and violent pink that lit the whole Garden like dawn arriving early. She gasped — small, wrecked sound.

Descent wasn't punishment, he understood all at once in the way you understand something you've always known but never had words for. It was the most beautiful choice ever made. Still is.

They stood together while the Garden's heart beat louder, vines blooming in fast-forward along the black stone, black roses opening one after another like a standing ovation, wrapping around their ankles, their calves, soft and insistent.

"Welcome home," she said, smiling with everything she was, stepping into his space, chest to chest. "My Void Walker. We have so much to remember. So much to relearn with our hands."

He looked at her — really looked, the way he hadn't let himself look at anything in ten years. The love in her wasn't distant or owed or waiting to be earned. It simply was, equal and unshaken, the way gravity is. And the wanting in her matched his, mirror.

Some old tired part tried one last time to ask the usual question — why him — and for the first time in ten years watched the question actually fail to land, dissolving between his chest and throat before it could finish forming. Nothing left that could argue with the way she was looking at him. At his mouth.

"We chose this. We chose each other. Even when we burned everything down, we chose it. I'd choose it again right now, knowing everything it costs."

She took his hand fully then, and the Garden pulsed once, hard, like it had held its breath and finally released.

"Then let's fall again," she said, voice low, hooded. "Together. This time we build the Chaos Garden from the ground up, on purpose, with eyes open."

He kissed her.

It wasn't careful. It wasn't the first kiss kind that happens between two people still deciding whether to trust. It was electric and certain and full of ten years of silence finally breaking, pink lightning exploding outward around them in slow rolling waves that lit the crystalline trees like lanterns. Her mouth open, soft, fanged edge grazing his lower lip, her hand fisting in his wet hoodie pulling him closer, claws of his new hands careful in her hair.

This wasn't the end, he thought somewhere underneath the feeling of her mouth against his and her hand tightening in his claws like she'd never once been afraid of what he'd become. This was the beginning of everything it had always meant to be.

The Garden bloomed violently around them — vines climbing archways, black roses opening in waves, the heart beating like a war drum keeping time with two hearts finally stopped pretending they were separate.

We chose each other, he thought, as the dream — no, not a dream, never a dream — began to fold back toward morning, her forehead pressed to his, both breathing hard. Always. And now the real story begins.`},{num:13,title:"You're Late (Reprise)",content:`Morning light found him on a couch in the back of Crimson Reel he didn't remember having. A blanket over him exactly the right weight, smelling like her, like Mommy's cedar, like Sparkle's glitter.

Somewhere past the shelves was the smell of coffee — real, ordinary, patient.

He sat up slowly, disoriented but not alarmed, and followed the smell out toward the front.

The red door stood in the corner where it always had, and he found himself standing in front of it from the inside now — a place he'd never once stood before. He noticed near its base some small handwriting he had almost missed.

Don't let her burn alone.

His own hand. Unmistakably his.

He crouched, studying it a long time, not with fear. With quiet reckoning. He touched the letters lightly, half-expecting them to smear or vanish. They didn't. Real, whatever that meant now.

"I don't know what I figured out," he said, when he turned and found Demikun already watching from the doorway, unbothered at being caught. Wearing his blue hoodie, too big, hem barely covering her thighs, hair messy from sleep.

"You're late," she said, easy, familiar, like she'd been waiting exactly as long as she expected to.

"For what?"

"I don't know yet. Felt right to say it anyway." Her smile was small and real, her eyes dragging over him slow, possessive, fond. "You stayed. That's enough for now. More than enough."

She turned back toward the counter and slid a cup of coffee across toward him — the same gesture from the first morning — except everything about it meant something different now. Their hands brushed. Lingering.

Outside, the rain kept falling, the same rain that greeted him on the worst night of the last decade — except the light through it had changed, warmer now, less grey, less like punishment and more like just weather. Like the world after being touched.

Across the street, his car still sat where he'd left it days ago. He looked through the window a long moment.

He didn't move toward the door.

Somewhere in the back of his mind, unbidden, a line of a song he'd abandoned months ago surfaced, and for the first time in longer than he could remember, it didn't come with the usual weight of failure attached. Just a thought. Just a small quiet maybe. Maybe I can want again.

He stayed exactly where he was, coffee warm in his hands, and let the moment be his without rushing to decide anything about it. Demikun watched him choose it — said nothing, didn't push, simply let him arrive at it in his own time, the way she'd been letting him arrive at everything since the night he walked back through her door. The way you let someone you love take their time.

The rain went on outside, unbothered, ordinary, entirely changed.

He stayed.

We chose each other, he thought. Always.`}];function ii(){let[e,t]=Oe.useState(1),[n,r]=Oe.useState(0),[l,u]=Oe.useState(!0),[o,i]=Oe.useState(!1),a=Oe.useRef(null),f=oi.find((p)=>p.num===e);return Oe.useEffect(()=>{let p=()=>{let v=a.current;if(!v)return;let m=v.getBoundingClientRect(),g=window.scrollY,S=document.documentElement.scrollHeight-window.innerHeight,k=S>0?g/S*100:0;r(Math.min(100,Math.max(0,k)))};return window.addEventListener("scroll",p,{passive:!0}),p(),()=>window.removeEventListener("scroll",p)},[e]),Oe.useEffect(()=>{window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:"smooth"})},[e]),L("div",{className:"min-h-screen bg-[#0a0a0f] text-[#e8e0e6] selection:bg-[#ff4da6]/30 selection:text-white antialiased relative overflow-x-hidden",children:[P("style",{children:`
